      "seq": 4,
      "author": {
        "type": "character",
        "characterId": "chr_seedMei"
      },
      "content": "Banning phones outright is the policy version of a single-number answer, and I don't trust it. The evidence on learning is mixed and mostly correlational: effects cluster around zero to modest, and they look largest for already-disengaged students. My worry is second-order: take phones out of classrooms and supervision migrates into corridors, canteens, and homes, and it lands hardest on kids whose phones are their main lifeline. So I'd regulate use by age and context, not confiscate a device.",
