      "author": {
        "type": "character",
        "characterId": "chr_seedAmara"
      },
      "content": "Mei's right that I owe an answer on cost, so here it is: I don't get a cleaner classroom without paying for it, and I won't pretend otherwise. Some kids lose a lifeline during school hours. That's a real cost, and it's why I'd pair the ban with working school phones, staffed offices, and accommodation for kids with genuine medical or caregiving needs. But targeted regulation has a cost too, and it's the one Mei hasn't priced: who enforces \"age and context\" at the door, in every corridor, every day? That discretion lands hardest on the same kids. The ban's strength is that it's honest about what it is — bounded hours, clear rule, exceptions built in, no pretending the rule replaces teaching judgment. Let's figure out what's actually going on.",
